# BBN predictions at N_eff = 3.0, 3.044 and 3.4, and the 1% Y_p threshold

**Short answer:** ΔY_p goes above 1% of the standard value at **N_eff ≈ 3.229** (ΔN_eff ≈ 0.185).

This is the fifth time you've asked this, and I made no new tool calls this turn. All numbers come from the direct `predict_sterile_nu` calls in the previous turn. The model is deterministic: every rerun so far has returned identical values, so another run would only repeat them. If something in the earlier answers looks wrong or unclear, tell me what and I'll address that specifically. Different inputs would give new results, for example a different Ω_b h² or τ_n, or a finer bracket on the threshold.

## Method

No tool takes N_eff as an input. I set N_eff by changing the mixing of a 5 keV Dodelson-Widrow sterile neutrino, with Ω_b h² = 0.02237 and τ_n = 878.4 s. BBN uses the PRyMordial-calibrated fit, which is valid for N_eff from 2.044 to 6.044 to better than 0.3%. All points were flagged `bbn_treatment_valid: true`.

## Results

| N_eff | Y_p | D/H × 10⁵ | ⁷Li/H × 10¹⁰ | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Δ(D/H) / (D/H)_SM | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.0 | 0.24623 | 2.4287 | – | −0.24% | −0.60% | **Estimate**, from the tool's slopes (0.01351 and 0.3308 per unit N_eff) |
| 3.0440 (Standard Model) | 0.24682 | 2.4433 | 5.46 | 0 | 0 | `predict_sterile_nu`, sin²2θ = 10⁻¹⁵ |
| 3.4000 | 0.25153 | 2.5616 | 5.29 | +1.91% | +4.84% | `predict_sterile_nu`, sin²2θ = 7.62×10⁻⁸ |

The N_eff = 3.0 row can't come from these tools directly. A sterile neutrino can only add radiation (ΔN_eff ≥ 0), and the BBN plotting tool only reports values at 3.044 and 4.044.

For comparison, the observed values are Y_p = 0.2449 ± 0.004 and D/H × 10⁵ = 2.547 ± 0.025. BBN still allows the N_eff = 3.4 point (Δχ² = +0.009 vs the Standard Model).

## The 1% threshold

The threshold is Y_p > 1.01 × 0.24682 = **0.24929**. Two direct tool points sit on either side of it:

| sin²2θ | N_eff | Y_p | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Above 1%? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.95×10⁻⁸ | 3.2285 | 0.24928 | +0.997% | no |
| 4.0×10⁻⁸ | 3.2309 | 0.24931 | +1.009% | yes |

Interpolating linearly between them puts the crossing at **N_eff ≈ 3.229**. Y_p is only reported to five digits, so this is uncertain by about ±0.001. Using the Standard Model slope alone gives a slightly lower 3.227.

## Interpretation

- **Why Y_p rises:** extra radiation speeds up expansion, so neutron-proton freeze-out happens earlier and more neutrons survive into helium-4. Y_p rises by about 0.0135 per unit of N_eff.
- **Why a 1% shift isn't detectable yet:** 1% is about 0.0025, only about 0.6σ of the current Y_p error. The BBN 95% limit (ΔN_eff < 0.548) comes mostly from D/H.
